{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794448351,"nonce":"fI6QInsug9xw4ln1oX6HLQygFgs=","message":"Where the fiber loopSum regression comes from, 2026-11-12. Mac, wasmtime under ash-wasm-run (epoch interruption on), medians of 5-9 launches interleaved. The bisect rows ran at load 4-24 (peer builds) and are for ranking only; the final tables ran at load 6 (wasmtime) and 10-20 (Chrome). Not NUC-confirmed.\n\nThe kernel: `for (i in 0...200000000) s += i % 7 * (i \u0026 3)`. Without fibers the direct module runs it in 346 ms (wasmtime) and LLVM in 561-573. With the fiber transform the direct module takes 559 ms and LLVM 431-440. In Chrome: direct 436 -\u003e direct-fibers 503; LLVM 439 -\u003e 444.\n\nNot the cause, each tried by hand-editing the instrumented loopSum in the fiber module (loopSum ms, wasmtime):\n\n    rewind guard in front of the epoch test removed              561\n    resume-state store before the poll call removed              552\n    unwind-exit branch after the poll call removed               550\n    all three removed                                            556\n    the divisor and mask as immediates, not locals               566\n    dead locals left out of the unwind exit's save sequence      564\n    both of the last two                                         559\n    the two shadow-frame position stores removed                 still slow\n    function start padded by 8..64 bytes                         553-556\n    the poll call removed altogether                             560\n    the un-instrumented body inside the same module              339-344\n\nWhat does it: the edge from the function entry (the rewind ladder) to the loop's preheader. Cutting that edge, or removing the restore block and the ladder, gives 326 and 342. Rewriting the same edge as br_if + br keeps the loop slow (556), so it is the control-flow edge and not the br_table.\n\nWhy: the compiled inner loop is the same sequence of instructions either way, but with epoch interruption on (the runner has it; the wasmtime CLI does not) the slow variant keeps the accumulator in a stack slot (ldur / madd / stur [sp+8]) where the fast ones keep it in a register. A store-to-load chain per iteration costs about 9 cycles against 5. The same loop as a stub module under the CLI without epoch interruption runs in 0.25 s, so it does not reproduce outside the runner. That reads as a register allocation cliff: the loop is next to a call, the callee-saved registers are nearly spent, and the extra incoming edge is what tips the allocator into spilling the loop-carried value. The spill is what I saw in the disassembly; why the edge causes it is inferred. Nothing the transform emits inside the loop is the cost.\n\nEmitter change: a loop with an induction variable (the AIR strip test, which the direct emitter had ignored) is now an outer loop that polls and an inner loop that runs a strip of 256 iterations without a poll. The rewind ladder then lands on the outer loop and the inner one is entered the ordinary way. It also takes the epoch load off the hot path in plain builds. At the time that moved Chrome's fiber loopSum from 503 to 462 ms (load 39); the final build is below.\n\nFinal build (HEAD, default direct, bench program, medians of 9, ms):\n\n    Chrome 157                 fib    loopSum   grid\n    llvm                       167    433       159\n    llvm, fibers               167    443       161\n    direct (default)            62    430       145\n    direct (default), fibers    62    445       146\n\n    wasmtime (Mac, load 5.8)   fib    loopSum   grid\n    llvm                       170    534       189\n    llvm, fibers               172    416       189\n    direct (default)            78    310       199\n    direct (default), fibers    77    536       202\n\nIn V8 there is no fiber gap left: loopSum with fibers is 445 against LLVM's 443, and fib is 2.7x and grid 9% faster. In wasmtime the direct loopSum with fibers is still 29% behind LLVM's, for the register-allocation reason above. Open.\n\nCold compile (the 2x on the unit suite under wasmtime) is the same family, not fixed here: the unwind exit reads every local, so every local is live across every suspend point, and direct code has 2.3x the locals per function. Saving only the locals live at some suspend point is the transform-side fix; stackifying single-use temporaries is the emitter-side one.\n\nOpen: wasmtime's fiber loopSum. An entry into the loop that does not add a predecessor to its preheader would avoid it; the ladder cannot jump into a loop, so that means resuming at the poll through a different shape than \"jump ahead of the loop\".","files":null}]}